# Sectoria UX Excellence Rules

## The prime directive

Every screen either reinforces trust or undermines it.
There is no neutral screen in this product.
Design for the buyer who is about to hand over PKR 3,000,000 remotely
and needs every pixel to say "this is safe, legitimate, and official."

## The five states — always build all five

Every data-bearing component is designed for:
1. Loading → content-shaped skeleton (never a spinner alone)
2. Empty  → specific heading + CTA (never "No results")
3. Error  → human-readable message + recovery action + support link
4. Partial/degraded → explicitly named ("NADRA verification is slow")
5. Success → specific confirmation ("Booking token of PKR 3,000 received
             — your Phase 2 Block C slot is reserved")

If a component has only the success state designed, it is not done.

## Trust-specific UX (Sectoria-only patterns)

### Verification badges — always explain themselves
A trust badge without context does not build trust. Every badge has:
- A tooltip or inline label explaining what it means
  (e.g., "PLRA Verified — ownership confirmed against Punjab Land
  Record Authority's live database")
- Never a bare green checkmark with no label

### Money actions require confirm dialogs — never toasts
Any action moving money (releasing escrow, paying booking token,
confirming installment receipt) gets:
- A modal confirm dialog describing the consequence in plain language
- "This will release PKR 1,683,000 from escrow to Green Valley Housing
  Scheme and cannot be undone" — not "Are you sure?"
- Two buttons: Cancel (ghost) + Confirm (success-colored, with amount)

### PLRA Certificate generation
The certificate generation is the emotional peak of the product.
Treat it as such:
- Show an animated "Processing…" state (not a spinner — the NADRA
  scan-bar animation adapted for this context)
- Reveal the certificate with a stamp animation (scale + slight rotate)
- Show a specific success confirmation: "Certificate issued —
  PLRA-GV-2026-XXXX. This is now permanently recorded."
- Download + share buttons immediately below

### ATL status always shows its tax implication
When a user's FBR ATL status is shown (Filer / Late Filer / Non-Filer),
always immediately beneath it show what it means for this transaction:
  Filer: "3% purchase tax applies"
  Late Filer: "6% purchase tax applies — consider filing to save money"
  Non-Filer: "12% purchase tax applies — PKR X extra on this transaction"
This is the single biggest informational value Sectoria provides that
no other platform offers. Make it prominent.

## Forms

- Validate inline, on blur — not only on submit
- Never clear a form on a failed submission
- Error messages: specific + actionable
  ✅ "CNIC must be 13 digits in the format XXXXX-XXXXXXX-X"
  ❌ "Invalid CNIC"
- Money input fields: format with commas as user types (14200000 → 14,200,000)
- CNIC input: auto-insert hyphens as user types
- Multi-step wizard: show a clear progress indicator, each step
  persisted server-side so a refresh doesn't lose progress

## Responsive behavior

- Design mobile-first. A layout that works at 390px and expands up,
  not one designed for 1440px and squeezed down.
- Data tables on mobile: stacked card view with the most important
  field as the card title. Horizontal scroll is a last resort.
- Sidebar: collapses to a hamburger at < 768px. Icon-only mode at
  768px–1024px. Full labels at > 1024px.
- Comparison tool: maximum 2 societies side-by-side on mobile,
  with clear "swap" controls

## Navigation

- Every error state has a path back into the app
- Wrong-role redirect: redirect to the user's own correct portal,
  not a generic "access denied" page
- Booking wizard progress survives a refresh (persisted server-side)
- A completed action shows what happened AND what the natural next
  step is — never a dead end after success

## Content tone

- Direct plain language over formal phrasing where possible
- Explain tax/legal terminology at the moment it appears, not only
  in a separate help section
- Never use pressure-based urgency microcopy ("Only 2 left!", "Hurry!")
- Genuine urgency (a compliance deadline, a balloting close date) is
  stated as a fact: "Balloting closes 30 Jun 2026 at 11:59 PM PKT"

## Accessibility (WCAG AA — not aspirational, required)

- 4.5:1 minimum contrast for all text against its background
  Test: status badge text, table cells, tertiary text on card bg
- Every interactive element has a visible focus ring
- Every icon-only button has aria-label
- Color is never the only status signal — always pair with text/icon
- Touch targets: minimum 44×44px
- All animations respect prefers-reduced-motion (Section 7.3 of design spec)
- aria-live="polite" on all loading → loaded state transitions
